Every movie was a spectacle that couldn't be recreated anywhere else. Back then, studios and theaters had a business arrangement, where if a theater had a certain movie, nobody else in the area could play it. Seeing a film was kind of like seeing a painting or a Broadway show. It lived at one particular location, and you had to go there. If you lived in the suburbs, you had to get in a car or get on the train and you had to go up to San Francisco to see the first run movie. And then afterwards, the movies would play later in the suburbs. In the early 70s, this began to change. Studios realized there was enough demand to release
每一部电影都是一种在其他任何地方都无法重现的奇观。那时，制片厂和电影院有一种商业安排，如果一家电影院放映某部电影，该地区的其他人就不能放映。看电影就像看一幅画或一场百老汇演出。它只在一个特定的地点存在，你必须去那里。如果你住在郊区，你必须开车或坐火车去旧金山看首轮电影。然后之后，电影才会晚些时候在郊区放映。70年代初，这种情况开始改变。制片厂意识到有足够的需求来同时在郊区和城市发行

